JavaScript inheritance is prototype-based.

Every ordinary object has an internal `[[Prototype]]` link whose value is another object or `null`. When code reads a property, JavaScript first checks the object's own properties. If the property is not found, it follows the `[[Prototype]]` link and continues until the property is found or the chain ends at `null`.

```javascript
const animal = {
  speak() {
    return `${this.name} makes a sound`;
  }
};

const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.name = "Rex";

dog.speak(); // Rex makes a sound
```

`name` is an own property of `dog`. `speak` is found on `animal`, which is in `dog`'s prototype chain.

Two related concepts have confusing names:

1. An object's internal `[[Prototype]]` is what property lookup follows. Inspect it with `Object.getPrototypeOf(object)`.
2. A constructor function's public `.prototype` property is the object assigned as the new instance's `[[Prototype]]` when the constructor is called with `new`.

```javascript
function Animal(name) {
  this.name = name;
}

Animal.prototype.speak = function () {
  return `${this.name} makes a sound`;
};

const dog = new Animal("Rex");

Object.getPrototypeOf(dog) === Animal.prototype; // true
```

Class syntax uses the same prototype model. Methods written in a class body normally live on `ClassName.prototype`, so instances share one function instead of receiving a separate copy.

Avoid using `obj.__proto__` in application code. The standard inspection API is `Object.getPrototypeOf()`, and prototypes should normally be established during object creation rather than changed repeatedly at runtime.

An object can intentionally have a `null` prototype:

```javascript
const dictionary = Object.create(null);
```

It does not inherit methods such as `toString` or `hasOwnProperty`, which can be useful for dictionary-like objects but requires using APIs such as `Object.hasOwn()`.

