blocks.explanation:
Class bodies use strict mode. Fix it by preserving the receiver:

```javascript
const handler = counter.increment.bind(counter);
```

or by calling through a wrapper:

```javascript
const handler = () => counter.increment();
```

An arrow class field is another option when stable callback identity is useful:

```javascript
increment = () => {
  this.count += 1;
};
```

That arrow function is created per instance, so it has a memory trade-off compared with one shared prototype method.

